spellin -- Command

Compress a sorted word list into a spell dictionary
spellin < wordlist > dictionary

spellin reads words from the standard input, one per line, and writes them to
the standard output in the packed form that spell(1) reads.  It takes no
options and no file arguments; a dictionary is built by redirection.

    sort -uf words.new /usr/dict/clista | spellin > /usr/dict/clista.new

THE INPUT MUST BE SORTED, and sorted the way spell searches: case-folded,
duplicates removed, one word per line.  spellin does not check this and does
not sort for you.  A dictionary built from unsorted input is not diagnosed --
it simply fails to find words, since spell walks it in collating order and
stops looking once it is past where the word should have been.

The Encoding
A word is packed three characters to sixteen bits, five bits each, with the
sixteenth bit marking the last group of a word.  The alphabet is the
twenty-six letters, which are folded to lower case, plus hyphen and
apostrophe; anything else in a word is encoded as zero, the pad the last group
of a short word is filled with.  Nothing in the packing depends on this
machine's byte order or word size, so a dictionary is portable to any COHERENT
system whose spell reads this format.

A `#' in the input word is the optional-s marker: spell expands such an entry
into both the singular and the plural, so `cat#' matches cat and cats and
takes one entry rather than two.

Diagnostics
    spellin: I/O error on output

The output could not be written -- normally a full filesystem.  Exit status is
1, and the partly-written dictionary must be discarded.  A usage message and
exit status 1 answer any argument at all.

Files
/usr/dict/clista	the American word list, in this format
/usr/dict/clistb	the British word list
/usr/lib/spell		the program that reads them

Neither dictionary is shipped and there is no word list here to build one
from, so spell answers `cannot open' on a fresh system until somebody feeds a
word list through spellin.  That is what this command is for.

Notes
This page has no COHERENT Lexicon article behind it.  The Lexicon documents
spell, and this system installs the dictionary builder as a program of its
own.  The page was written here from the source in base/cmd/spell; none of
Mark Williams Company's text is in it.

This is the sorted-list spell, not the hashed one, and its dictionaries are
therefore ordinary word lists that sort -m can merge before spellin packs
them.  A dictionary from a hashed spell is a different file entirely and
cannot be read here.

